[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a minute if you need it, then tell me how you'd structure your approach.

[candidate · 1:30] Let me take a second to lay this out.

Since revenue is actually growing but margin is collapsing, this looks cost-driven, but I want to confirm both sides before I commit.

I'd structure it as profit = revenue minus cost.

On revenue: I'd break it into number of transactions times average ticket, and split that by same-store versus new-store growth. A chain that's opened a lot of locations can grow 15% on the top line while per-store economics worsen.

On cost: I'd split fixed versus variable. Variable would be COGS — coffee beans, milk, cups — plus hourly labor. Fixed would be rent, salaried store management, corporate overhead, and any depreciation from new builds.

Then I'd compare the two years as a percent of revenue to see which line moved.

To start — do we know whether the 15% revenue growth is same-store or from new locations?
