[interviewer · CLARIFY · 1:34] 200 stores today — and it was 200 stores two years ago, so the footprint hasn’t changed. Your framework has several branches. Hold that request; I'll come back to it shortly. Which one do you prioritize first, and why?

[candidate · 3:04] That's useful — flat footprint means the 15% is same-store, so volume or ticket is genuinely up. That makes a demand problem unlikely.

So I'd prioritize the cost side first, specifically cost as a percent of revenue by line item across the two years.

My reasoning: margin fell 18 points on growing revenue. If revenue is up 15% and we're still losing that much margin, costs have to be growing meaningfully faster than revenue. Revenue investigation would tell me how we grew, but it won't explain the decline.

Within cost, I'd look at variable costs first — COGS and hourly labor — because those scale with volume, and volume is up. But I'd want to see whether they rose more than proportionally, which would point to input price inflation or wage pressure rather than just more cups sold.

Do we have a cost breakdown as a percent of revenue for both years?
