[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 9:21] Understood. Beyond what we've discussed, what else could the client do to reverse the decline? Give me a structured set of ideas.

[candidate · 10:51] A few buckets.

On price: raise menu prices, since volume is up 15% and we have no evidence of demand weakness. Could be across-the-board or targeted at the least price-sensitive items.

On procurement: renegotiate or lock in longer-term bean contracts, hedge forward, consolidate suppliers for volume discounts, or diversify origin to cheaper growing regions.

On product: reformulate or re-engineer the menu — shift mix toward lower-bean-intensity drinks, resize portions, or push higher-margin food and non-coffee items.

On waste and yield: check dosing consistency, spoilage on dairy, and shrink. If per-unit bean cost is up 40%, every gram wasted costs more than it used to.

On overhead: it moved 2 points, which is small but worth understanding.

I'd note the pricing lever is the most direct, since the gap is literally that prices haven't moved while inputs have.
